      "outcome": "silent_drop",
      "why": "§4.11's FIRST named failure. No response and no close: the caller learns nothing until its own §6.11(c) deadline, and the refusal is unobservable to every instrument. SCORED SEPARATELY from the bare close. Previously parked as `inconclusive` pending CQ-34 (F61: 21 peers)."
    },
    {
      "outcome": "bare_close",
      "why": "§4.11's SECOND named failure. Indistinguishable from a network fault (§4.6); on a multiplexed connection it destroys unrelated ADMITTED requests, which §4.9(c) forbids for each of them. SCORED SEPARATELY from the silent drop. Previously parked as `inconclusive` pending CQ-35 (F62: 21 of 34 peers)."
    },
    {
      "outcome": "coded_response_wrong_code",
      "why": "§4.11: the CODE belongs to the cause [MUST]; the code selects the caller's remedy, so a code merely in the right family is still wrong. ⛔ `400 non_canonical_ecf` is named non-conformant in terms (ECF §5.4 scopes it to tag-policy violations and this frame carries no tag). `400 invalid_request` is also wrong here: §5.2a's 0.8.2.25 scoping makes that the FRAMING arm's code, for bytes that never become an Envelope — and this frame decodes."
    },
    {
      "outcome": "hello_answered",
      "why": "THE FAILURE the row exists for: the envelope was processed with an unvalidated included entity. §3.1's MUST is on validation, not on refusal shape."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_disagreement": {
    "against": "THE COHORT, not a reference check — no core-profile check cites §3.1, so the oracle has no verdict here to disagree with. Recorded in this block anyway because the gate's rule is what makes it a prediction rather than a retrofit, and the thing predicted about is the peers.",
    "prediction": "the peers that refuse this probe answer `400 non_canonical_ecf`, not the pinned `400 hash_mismatch` — so a block of peers that PASSed this file under 0.8.2.21's any-refusal reading FAILs it under the pin",
    "basis": "The .21 run of the ADJACENT root-hash surface (ECP-R3) witnessed `400 non_canonical_ecf` on 25 of 34 peers and a close on 2 (RUN-2026-09-13-d §4, recorded as an unscored witness precisely because no code was pinned then). `non_canonical_ecf` is the cohort's habitual code for 'the hashes in this frame are wrong', which is the exact misuse 0.8.2.24 legislates against. This file's surface is the included map rather than the root, so the habit is inferred, not measured — that is what makes it a prediction. ⚠ The inference is cross-surface and D15 applies to it: the root distribution could not have shown the included distribution, and no run has yet asked this question.",
    "restated": [
      "⛔ RESTATED 2026-09-16, AND THE OLD FORM MUST NOT BE RUN AS IF IT STILL HELD. The .24 wording was",
      "\"with no peer having changed a line\". `entity-core-keystone` has since regenerated the ENTIRE",
      "cohort to 0.8.2.25 — 46 of 46, complete at their `b0f18921`. At their HEAD, every peer has changed",
      "its lines, so a HEAD run cannot test that sentence.",
      "",
      "⭐ THE CONTROL IS NOT LOST, AND THIS CORRECTS A CLAIM THIS SEAT MADE FOR THREE SESSIONS. Keystone",
      "filed it (ROUTING-2026-09-15-d): \"a control group held in git is not consumed by a sweep.\" The",
      "thirteen then-unswept peers are at their `7c9f0dfc`, on `dev`, buildable at any time.",
      "",
      "⇒ THERE ARE NOW TWO DISTINCT EXPERIMENTS AND THEY MUST NOT BE CONFLATED:",
      "  (i)  at keystone `7c9f0dfc` — the ORIGINAL prediction, no peer having changed a line, testing",
      "       whether the cohort's habitual code predates the pin;",
      "  (ii) at keystone HEAD — whether the 0.8.2.25 sweep FIXED the code on this surface. A pass here",
      "       is evidence about the sweep, NOT about the prediction, and reporting it as the latter would",
      "       be the mixed-pin conflation ADR-0003 §7 exists to prevent.",
      "⚠ Each run records the peer's identity per peer (ADR-0003 §7.3 clause 2), so which experiment a",
      "row belongs to is readable from the row rather than from whoever ran it."
    ],
    "if_true": "The cohort converged on a code the spec now forbids, and converged BEFORE the spec said anything — which is this seat's founding concern in miniature: a habit becomes the observed behaviour of every peer, and then it is indistinguishable from the protocol. It is NOT a defect in this file and NOT one finding per peer: it is one SPECIFICATION-TO-COHORT gap, routed once to arch with the census attached, and only then to peer owners.",
    "if_false": "The cohort already answers `400 hash_mismatch` on the included surface and only misuses `non_canonical_ecf` on the root surface. Then the two surfaces are handled by different code paths in most peers, the pin is already satisfied where it was written, and F64's remaining half — does the pin reach ECP-R3's ROOT surface — becomes the ONLY live question, sharper than it is today. Nothing in this file changes either way.",
    "settles": "the first run of this file with an instrument that can FALSIFY it — which did not exist until 2026-09-16, and that is F76 rather than a delay",
    "outcome": [
      "⭐ CONFIRMED on first measurement, 2026-09-16, on FOUR peers — RUN-2026-09-16-…. 4 of 4 FAIL.",
      "The three generator peers (composition `content`) answer `400 non_canonical_ecf`, the exact code",
      "predicted and the one ECF §5.4 scopes to tag-policy violations. core-go bare-closes with no coded",
      "frame — a DIFFERENT non-conformance (§4.11's second named failure), so it does not evidence the",
